    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Form-upload version (n8n on server, files on your PC)\n\nn8n runs on the server, so it can't read your laptop's files. This version takes the HTML\n**through your browser** and hands the Excel back as a download \u2014 no server file access.\n\n**Why the last node is an n8n Form node:** this n8n build blocks the \"Respond to Webhook\"\nnode in Form-Trigger workflows, so the response is produced by the **\"Download Excel\"** node\n(n8n Form node, Page Type = *Form Ending*, Respond With = *Return Binary File*). Leave the\nForm Trigger's \"Respond When\" at its default \u2014 do **not** set it to \"Using Respond to Webhook.\"\n\n**How to use**\n1. Open \"On form submission\" and copy its form URL (Test URL while editing; Production URL\n   once the workflow is Active).\n2. In your browser, open it, pick the dashboard `.html`, and submit.\n3. The completion page returns the filtered Excel as a download.\n\n**Config**\n- `ownerList` \u2014 your 5 owners (prefilled). Edit in the Config node.\n\n**Notes**\n- Columns: Overdue Tickets, Ticket URL, Severity, Assignment, State, Summary (plain text;\n  a clickable `=HYPERLINK` needs the production SharePoint/Graph version).\n- \"Normalize upload\" auto-detects the uploaded file's binary property, so parsing just works.\n- If the file that downloads is the HTML you uploaded rather than the Excel, that's an older\n  Form-Ending quirk with binary selection \u2014 update n8n, or ask me for the variant that forces\n  the correct binary.\n",
        "height": 640,
        "width": 500,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "30621344-e455-476f-b9b3-6b586f64db70",
      "name": "Setup",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        240,
        560
      ]
    }
